/** Standalone batch U -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample (uva-165 and uva-240 each verified against MULTIPLE cases within a single
 * sample, giving extra cross-validation confidence) before being trusted to generate expected output.
 *
 * uva-122-trees-on-the-level: build each tree from its (value,path) pairs into a path->value map;
 *   "not complete" if any path is given twice, or any node's parent path was never given a value.
 *   Level-order-left-to-right output falls out for free from sorting paths by (length, then plain
 *   string comparison) since 'L' < 'R' in ASCII already matches left-before-right at each depth.
 * uva-141-the-spot-game: track the occupied-cell set after each move; a board pattern's canonical
 *   form is the lexicographically smallest of its 4 rotations (0/90/180/270, via (r,c) -> (c,N+1-r)
 *   repeated), so a duplicate is detected even when the repeat is only a ROTATION of an earlier
 *   pattern, not a literal re-occurrence -- both the boundary and tricky case are built specifically
 *   around this (two different literal corners collapsing to the same canonical single-corner class).
 * uva-145-gondwanaland-telecom: split each call's [start,end) minute interval across at most 2
 *   calendar-day cycles (calls can wrap past midnight and last up to 24h) and overlap each day-cycle
 *   segment against the day/evening/night minute ranges; charges computed in integer cents to avoid
 *   float rounding drift. Calls under 1 minute are silently dropped from the output entirely (not
 *   printed as a zero line), per the problem statement.
 * uva-160-factors-and-factorials: Legendre's formula for each prime's exponent in N!; output format
 *   is a fixed 6-character "%3d! =" prefix followed by "%3d" per exponent, 15 per line, continuation
 *   lines indented by exactly that same 6-character prefix width (verified character-for-character
 *   against the real sample's line-wrap behavior, including the fencepost case of exactly 15 vs 16
 *   primes).
 * uva-165-stamps: for a FIXED (h,k), search denomination sequences a_1=1 < a_2 < ... < a_k by
 *   backtracking, where each new a_i is only ever tried in the range (a_{i-1}, R+1] with R = the max
 *   continuous value reachable (>=h stamps DP) using denominations chosen so far -- any candidate
 *   beyond R+1 would leave an unfillable gap at R+1 forever, and this range bound is what keeps the
 *   search tractable for h+k<=9. The stress case cross-validates against the SAME known-good values
 *   appearing in the real official sample (e.g. h=2,k=3 -> 8; h=3,k=2 -> 7; h=4,k=3 -> 26) at several
 *   points in a full h+k<=9 sweep, not just the sample's own pairs.
 * uva-240-variable-radix-huffman-encoding: pad the source alphabet with zero-frequency fictitious
 *   letters up to the smallest N' = R + m*(R-1) >= N, then repeatedly group the R lowest-(freq,rank)
 *   nodes per pass (rank = earliest REAL letter index in the node; fictitious-only nodes sort after
 *   every real letter) assigning target digits 0..R-1 ascending by (freq,rank); each letter's final
 *   code is its per-pass assigned digits IN REVERSE chronological order, per the problem statement.
 *   Verified against all 9 cases in the real official sample, including R=2 (plain binary Huffman)
 *   through R=10.
 * uva-242-stamps-and-envelope-size: for each of N given (not searched) denomination sets, compute its
 *   max continuous reach via the same reachability DP as uva-165, then pick the best set with the
 *   documented tie-break chain (max coverage, then fewest denominations, then lexicographically
 *   smallest by comparing denominations from the LARGEST down). Tricky case exercises multiple tiers
 *   of that tie-break chain against sets whose actual computed coverage (not just naive assumption)
 *   determines the winner.
 */
